From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nabeel Sowan <nabeel@vibes.se>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 21:25:28 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] displayconfig: offer to mirror the primary monitor on another
 monitor

The Displays module could not mirror at all. Its layout rules keep every
monitor from overlapping another, so a projector or a docked laptop's
panel could only extend the desktop, and pressing Apply on an
arrangement mirrored outside TDE pulled it apart. libtderandr applies
overlapping outputs correctly; only the module stood in the way.

Add "Mirror the primary monitor onto this monitor" under the existing
primary and extend options. A mirror is a non-primary monitor that is
active and placed at the primary monitor's position, so no new profile
field is needed: ticking the box moves the monitor there, unticking it
puts it to the right of the primary, and an arrangement that is already
mirrored is shown with the box ticked. The layout rules and dragging
leave a mirror alone. Each monitor keeps its own resolution, so a
smaller one shows the top left of the primary's image, the way xrandr
places two outputs at one position.

Tested on an Xorg dummy-driver server, primary 1024x768, second monitor
800x600 beside it, with the dialog accepted and checked after its
revert timeout:

- ticking the box gives a 1024x768 screen with both monitors at +0+0;
- with both already at +0+0, the box shows ticked and Apply keeps them
  there, where the unchanged module re-laid them to a 1024x1360 screen;
- unticking returns the second monitor to +1024+0.
